FINDINGS

1. FAIL — ACCESSIBILITY.md #7 ("UI component boundaries ... ≥ 3:1 against
   background") and #12 ("all contrast requirements (7) hold in both
   themes"); WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast / 2.4.11 Focus Appearance.
   web/static/js/charts.js:238,308 (both renderMajorExpenseBreakdown and
   renderMajorExpenseCredits rows) give the new `<button>` rows
   `hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-700 focus:outline-none
   focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-indigo-500`. This is a NEW
   idiom combination, not the pre-existing one: every other focusable
   element in the app (e.g. this same file's sort-header buttons,
   kpi-month-detail.html:69/77/85/93) pairs the ring with a hover TEXT
   color change only (`hover:text-gray-900 dark:hover:text-gray-100`),
   never a hover BACKGROUND change — `grep -rn
   "dark:hover:bg-gray-700.*focus-visible:ring-indigo-500"` finds no
   pre-existing occurrence anywhere else in web/templates or
   web/static/js; the combination is unique to this diff.
   Computed contrast (indigo-500 ring `rgb(99 102 241)`, confirmed via
   `grep -o '\.focus-visible\\:ring-indigo-500...'
   web/static/css/tailwind.css`, against the served hex values for
   gray-700/`rgb(55 65 81)` confirmed the same way):
     - ring vs card bg white (light, no hover):      4.47:1  PASS
     - ring vs hover bg gray-50 (light + hover):      4.27:1  PASS
     - ring vs card bg gray-800 (dark, no hover):      3.29:1  PASS (thin margin)
     - ring vs hover bg gray-700 (dark + hover):       2.31:1  FAIL (< 3:1)
   A keyboard user who tabs to a row while the mouse cursor is already
   resting on it (a real, not merely theoretical, combined state — e.g.
   shift-tab cycling back onto a row under the pointer) sees the focus
   ring at 2.31:1 against its own hover background in dark mode, below
   the constitution's unconditional 3:1 floor. The light-theme and
   dark-theme no-hover states pass; only the dark hover+focus compound
   state fails, so this is not a "control invisible in one theme" fail,
   but it is a real, in-scope, newly-introduced contrast deficiency
   attributable to this diff (not present in master — confirmed absent
   from `git diff master` baseline classes).

CONFIRMED PASSING (no failure)

2. CB6-3 keyboard/semantics: rows are real `<button type="button">`
   (charts.js:236,306), added via `document.createElement('button')` +
   `.type = 'button'`, not div-onclick — satisfies ACCESSIBILITY.md #3.
   `triggerMajorExpenseDrilldown` (charts.js:195-199) contains no keydown
   handling and no `preventDefault`/`stopPropagation` — native button
   Enter/Space activation is untouched, so keyboard operability
   (ACCESSIBILITY.md #9) holds without a separate keydown handler. The
   guard exactly mirrors the pre-existing donut wedge handler's
   `typeof openMajorExpenseDrilldown === 'function'` check
   (charts.js:178, unchanged) — same idiom, not a new failure mode.
   No nested interactive elements: row buttons contain only `<span>`
   phrasing content, valid per the HTML5 button content model.

3. CB6-3 accessible name: the button's accessible name is computed from
   ALL its text-content descendants (name span + amount text node [+
   percent span for the breakdown rows]), e.g. "Streaming $42.50  3.2%,
   button" — the visible group name is a strict subset of the accessible
   name (WCAG 2.5.3 Label in Name is satisfied a fortiori). Including the
   amount is not itself a violation; it is arguably more informative for
   screen-reader users than name-only. No color-only signaling is
   introduced by these rows (no color conveys the drilldown affordance).

4. CB6-4 sign-aware Total tile: enumerated handleKPIMonthDetail's switch
   (handlers.go:768-799) — only income / expenses / living / healthcare /
   default(savings, savings-rate, via IsSavings) exist; no kind falls
   outside the spec's enumeration, so nothing was guessed. Rendered-byte
   verification (dump harness, see METHOD #4) confirms:
     expenses  Total=+42.50 → text-red-600 dark:text-red-400,  "$42.50"
     expenses  Total=-42.50 → text-green-700 dark:text-green-400, "-$42.50"
     living    Total=+71.75 → red;  living Total=0.00 → green, "$0.00"
     healthcare Total=+210.00 → red
     income    Total=+3000.00 → green (unchanged)
     savings   Total=+100.00 → green (untouched IsSavings branch, ~line 30)
     savings   Total=-100.00 → red   (untouched IsSavings branch)
   All match the CB6-4 contract exactly; the class pairs (red-600/red-400,
   green-700/green-400) are the file's pre-existing, unmodified pairs
   (row-level convention at ~line 107, IsSavings Net tile at ~line 30).
   Contrast (hand-computed from served tailwind.css hex values, against
   the Total tile's actual container background bg-gray-50/dark:bg-gray-900):
     red-600 vs gray-50:    4.62:1  PASS (large text needs 3:1; text-xl
     green-700 vs gray-50:  4.80:1  PASS  font-bold = 20px bold = "large")
     red-400 vs gray-900:   6.41:1  PASS
     green-400 vs gray-900: 10.18:1 PASS
   axe-core found 0 color-contrast violations across all 8 rendered
   kind/sign combinations in both themes (METHOD #5).

5. CB6-4 color-only signaling: `formatMoney` (internal/templates/render.go:
   425-453) prepends a literal "-" for negative values ("-$42.50"), so the
   sign is textually present in every case the color differs, independent
   of color — satisfies ACCESSIBILITY.md #8. (At exactly Total=$0.00 there
   is no sign glyph, but zero is neither a debit nor a credit and carries
   no color-alone ambiguity to resolve.)

6. CB6-2 header wording: plain textContent change
   ("Net credits (refunds met or exceeded spending)"), same node, no
   markup/ARIA implications, no accessibility impact.

7. Confirmed no other markup changed: `git diff 0a697b1 --stat` plus a
   `python3 difflib` byte-diff of both touched files individually — the
   handlers.go and trends_test.go changes are non-UI (sort-order-only
   arithmetic path and a Go table test, respectively).

